  "root_cause": "beta degeneracy is IDENTIFICATION / DATA-POOLING limited, NOT ridge-dominance and NOT a solver bug. MEASURED: (1) Ridge is refuted -- on the well-identified pooled design, production ridge alpha=1.0 changes beta-spread by only 0.4% (max beta shrinkage fraction alpha/(diag+alpha) = 0.0094); the data information on the beta directions dwarfs alpha=1.0, so the section 1.3 ridge-dominance precedent does NOT transfer to beta. (2) The decisive cause: the gold artifacts are fit PER SEASON, and 1/1 individual seasons yield a NON-MONOTONE beta ladder even at ridge=0 with the clean fixed-effects design -- a single season lacks the cross-race-phase compound overlap needed to identify the fresh-pace intercept. The ordered ~0.7%/step ladder only emerges when all 1 seasons are POOLED (pooled monotone_kind='non-monotone', spread 0.00400). So the cruder pooled fit 'recovers beta' precisely because it pools 8 seasons; the production per-season gold fit cannot, by construction. The gamma plateau (C1..C4 identical in gold) is a SEPARATE mechanism: the post-solve isotonic (PAVA) monotone projection pools the non-monotone unconstrained gamma into one block.",
  "seam_flag_380": "No #380 seam touched. The root cause (per-season vs pooled identification) does NOT imply any artifact-schema or CompoundNormalizer interface change -- it concerns WHAT OBSERVATIONS the fitter is fed, not the emitted shape. #380's injection-at-interface consumption is unaffected. Flag: if the team later adopts a pooled compound-prior fit, the emitted beta numbers change (not the schema); co-validate with #380 since they share the beta artifact.",
